<%- include('../partials/info-start', { crumb: 'Trust', eyebrow: 'About the demo', heading: 'What’s real, and what’s simulated.', intro: 'Willow is designed to feel like a real bank. This page is the plain-language guide to where the line sits.' }) %>
            <section class="info-section" id="summary">
                <h2>At a glance</h2>
                <table class="info-table">
                    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Area</th><th scope="col">Real</th><th scope="col">Simulated</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Your profile</strong></td><td>Sign-in, password hashing, two-step verification, sessions, audit log</td><td>Identity verification at sign-up</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Accounts</strong></td><td>Ledger records, statements and history you can export</td><td>All balances, deposits, withdrawals and interest</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Cards</strong></td><td>Freeze and control settings stored on your profile</td><td>Card numbers, wallets and every card payment</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Payments</strong></td><td>Transfers between Willow profiles update both ledgers</td><td>Nothing reaches a bank, card network or payment rail</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Currencies</strong></td><td>Rates derived from delayed market data when available</td><td>Every conversion</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Investing and crypto</strong></td><td>Delayed prices, charts, company profiles and news</td><td>Every order and holding — funded only from money you deposit</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Loans and business</strong></td><td>Calculator maths</td><td>Applications, approvals, invoices and team invitations</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>
            <section class="info-section" id="try">
                <h2>Trying it out</h2>
                <p>Open a profile with fictional details, or start a guest profile from the homepage. Every profile — guest or not — starts empty: one checking account at $0, no cards, no history. Balances, budgets, debts and charts fill in only from what you do.<% if (guestRetentionDays) { %> Unused guest profiles are deleted after <%= guestRetentionDays %> days unless you save your own email and password.<% } %></p>
                <p>To try payments, open a second profile (or ask a friend to) — money only moves between real Willow profiles.</p>
            </section>
            <section class="info-section" id="market">
                <h2>Market data</h2>
                <p>A small Python service retrieves delayed quotes, price history, company profiles and headlines through the open-source yfinance library, and caches them briefly. If it can’t reach the data source, Willow shows “Market data temporarily unavailable.” rather than an estimate. Demo trades are blocked until fresh prices return.</p>
            </section>
            <section class="info-section" id="assistant">
                <h2>The assistant</h2>
                <p>Ask Willow uses a language model running on the same computer as Willow, through <a class="text-link" href="https://ollama.com" rel="noopener">Ollama</a>. With each question it receives a short summary of your own records — balances, recent activity, budgets, debts, net worth, investing and business figures — and nothing from anyone else. It doesn’t browse the web, predict markets or recommend trades. When Ollama isn’t running, the assistant is hidden.</p>
            </section>
            <section class="info-section" id="labels">
                <h2>Labels you’ll see</h2>
                <p>Look for the <span class="demo-badge">Demo</span> badge in the app, “Simulated” notes on receipts, and “Delayed” on prices. A full list is on the <a class="text-link" href="/compliance#labels">disclosures page</a>.</p>
            </section>
